u know hit the rack go to sleep get up  the next morning do it again for a while  so  that's what i would do and  i didn't just go to philosophy  conferences because i had an interest in  political theory both in terms of thomas  hobbes and  more contemporary stuff and also  aristotle i would go to political theory  conferences and one of the biggest  is the midwest political science  association  makes the you know national philosophy  uh the american philos philosophical  association their eastern  conference the eastern division  conference look like nothing because  there's so many people doing political  theory so i would go to that not least  because it was located every year in  chicago there's  a hotel that gets used by a lot of  conferences the palmer house hilton  right downtown very expensive of course  so i never actually stayed there um and  and i would go to conferences there and  and  i went to quite a few including the  central apa  i went to the midwest political science  association conferences there  and those would go on for four days and  i would drive  up from northwestern indiana  up to chicago taking you know the skyway  and parking downtown and all that it was  kind of a fun thing  and i every year that i applied i i got  a paper in so that was  really quite cool for me  and i would be on these panels and one  of the things that was really  interesting for me was seeing how  different disciplines would do this this  whole paper presenting thing  in philosophy you get your paper  generally and you've got it in front of  you and you just basically read it word  for word  although that's changing a bit these  days and in political  theory or political science it doesn't  work like that at all  on a panel you will have anywhere from  like  three to five main presenters  and each of them gets anywhere from like  you know  10 to maybe 20 minutes to present their  entire paper  the paper itself might be 40 pages long  which would take a very long time to  read and then you have  several respondents whose job is to read  the other people's papers and then say  something intelligent about it later  and then you have a chair and the chair  is supposed to be the person like  keeping track of everything making sure  nobody goes beyond their time  introducing everybody  and in general like being almost like  the ambassador  and mc for the whole session so it's  quite a performance  and it was you know a lot of fun to  participate in these things i got to  meet a lot more people at the political  science conferences because the panels  would be much larger in philosophy  they might be two to three people this  you're guaranteed to have like  five to seven people on a panel and some  of them can be a bit standoffish  particularly if they're older professors  and they think that you know this is  just like  doing some service but i met a lot of  cool people doing that  there's one session that i wanted to  tell a story about  because it was so funny  and also in some respects typical  of the sort of person who who  did this academic faux pas so  it was a panel and if i remember right  it was about the state of nature in  general so there were papers on  thomas hobbs i presented a paper on  thomas hobbs and then there was  there was stuff on lock and i think a  few other  early modern theorists and the chair was  one of the people presenting and that  was kind of weird right to begin with  because usually  the chair doesn't present the chair is  there to just sort of run things  but this guy was sort of a big name kind  of a big  muckety mock he was from the london  school of economics which  if you don't know is rather prestigious  compared to many other places  and people who work in those sorts of  elite institutions  often think that you know they matter  more than everybody else  and you know why wouldn't they everybody  else talks to them as if they do  and so they get kind of swelled heads  about about themselves and  that was the case with this guy and it  wasn't just being from the london school  of economics or talking with his posh  british  british accent or you know the way that  he sort of like kind of like  just ran his eyes over us and was like  okay that's that's  what we're gonna do today there was  something that happened a couple times  during the actual um  the the the panel presentation that  really stuck  stood out and and uh i'll tell you about  what happened and what he did and our  reactions  and then you know maybe muse a little  bit about what went on  so everybody was doing their papers you  know and then it came to his point where  he's like actually doing his paper  and so this would be like the early  2000s like 2004  2005 2006. and he's reading his paper  the little press see that he's made of  it beforehand right he's not reading the  entire thing  and his cell phone goes off and back  then cell phones were not the  smartphones they're you know usually  these flip phones  and you know okay so somebody's cell  phone going off that that  became a kind of constant thing that  would happen in movie theaters and  you know plays and stuff like that some  people just forget to turn their cell  phones off it's there's really nothing  wrong with  it now what do you do when your cell  phone goes off  if you're a decent civilized human being  who is respectful of others time and the  circumstances that you're in you know  you might look at it and be like oh i  don't have to i don't have to take this  call just turn it off you know  well instead he actually answered it so  he stopped his  his presentation and he didn't even say  anything like excuse me or anything like  that  just looked at his cell phone answered  it and we thought oh this must be like a  major emergency like his house is on  fire  or his kid fell down a well or who knows  what it is  and he just starts chatting with the  person and  we're all looking at each other like  what the hell is th